t. 
|  Since most judges tend to follow the recommendations of state CPS
|  agencies, who form close relationships with judges in their day-to-day
|  handling of neglect and abuse cases (and have political influence over
|  judicial appointments and reappointments as well - see the examples
|  previously discussed in this chapter), a parent whose position is opposed
|  by a CPS agency faces extraordinary obstacles. Even worse, this procedure can result in the state literally underwriting one party’s legal fees,
|  expert witness bills, and court transcript costs in a private custody action
|  against the other parent. It is difficult to imagine such an arrangement
.  in any other sort of litigation between two parties

--- Sentence/end 
